okay so you want to be a good  residential architect  but you've kind of lost your way here  are my five  tips to get you back on track so tip  number one  is relatively simple let me come around  to the couch  is listen to your client if you're able  to listen to your client and genuinely  pay attention to what they have  to say you're going to get a much better  outcome  both for yourself and for them now i say  it this way  because we don't want to redraw his  architect 100 times to get it right  and some of the time it is genuinely our  fault for not listening to the client  so if you're able to listen to what they  have to say and  interpret and understand what they've  said  you're much more likely to be able to  design something  that is exactly what they're looking for  now as well as being able to listen to  the client  very intensively it's also your job  to communicate with them on a very  personal and humble level  residential clients especially luxury  residential clients  pay a lot of money to be able to deal  with an architect one-on-one and to be  able to get  your insight and your knowledge so if  you're able to communicate with your  client  on a very personal level get to know  them get to know their marriage get to  know their family  this is going to result in a much better  outcome for both you  and the client and your next client  thanks to the great marketing strategy  of word of mouth  okay so we completely skipped the intro  so what's going on guys  my name is david thomich and welcome  back to the channel i've already spoken  about it and today we are talking about  top five tips for residential architect  the tip number three is very very  critical  and it is important to understand from  the get-go point number three  is understanding budget if you're able  to understand budget you're going to be  able to successfully design something  that the client wants  and be able to retain that all the way  through to build  the last thing you want to do is for  your client to come to you  and say hey david i've got a budget of 1  million dollars  sweet this is going to be a good project  and then you turn around and design them  a 4.5 million dollar mansion that they  can't afford it goes out  tender it gets tendered and the builders  give you that ridiculous price point  one your client is gonna be furious two  you're gonna have to redraw all that  for free and three you're gonna look  like an idiot so  make sure you understand budget and  understand it well  if your client comes to you with that  one million dollar price tag or even  900 000 for argument's sake and you know  in the back your head  the last project you did which is  roughly the same cost about  three thousand dollars a square meter  this is a figure i'm pulling out of my  head  don't use it in real life then you know  that you can go ahead and design  roughly a 300 square meter property  for these clients that's going to cost  900 000  to build when it comes back from tender  it might come in  just under just over but a 10  contingency  is kind of normal in the industry so if  you're  able to be able to design within the  budget within the contingency  you're going to have very very happy  clients now for argument's sake  this is where you actually get paid to  do your job with that three thousand  dollars a square meter we go on to tip  number four  be innovative you need to be innovative  where you spend the money  you don't want to go and blow all your  money on some  ridiculous marble staircase that's  tucked behind the corner of a lift shaft  you want to be able to spend that money  where people are going to see it and  enjoy it  so make sure that you go out and  uniquely  and thoroughly design every space so you  get the best bang for your buck  i know that's a generic saying but it is  very important to be innovative  in design and when it comes to budget  and last but not least  is tip number five as you've probably  already become aware  working with residential clients is very  personal it means you spend a lot of  time getting to know them and they spend  a lot of time getting to know you  so something that you may inadvertently  do is over promise  that's something everybody does every  now and again and as long as you can  deliver on time  it's okay but you're going to come to a  point in time where something goes wrong  and you can't deliver and a client's  going to be upset  so make sure you always under promise  and over deliver  it sounds very simple but it is very  difficult to do in practice  especially when you're running 10 to 20  different jobs concurrently so if you  can under promise and over deliver to  your clients they're going to be very  very happy  and you're going to seem like the hero  every single time that being said if you  need to tell your client you need a week  to do this job tell them you need a week  and a half  i know you're probably not going to need  that week and a half but it gives you  that little bit of  relaxed time just in case anything goes  wrong anyway that's all for me today  guys thank you so much for watching the  video i hope you enjoyed it  if you did make sure you smash that like  button down below help this  video with the youtube algorithm if you  want to see more of my content  smash that subscribe button down below  because as always  i will see you next monday
 

